# The map: the nodes and the shared places, drawn where they are rather than listed.
#
# What this scene is for is the d-pad. It is the only screen in the client where the four
# directions move the picture instead of a cursor, and that is not a thing a still can show - so
# the sequence below aims the crosshair by panning, which is what "aiming is panning" looks like
# from the outside.
#
# The places are put at nodes that are already in the demo roster, so every distance on the panel
# is measured between two real fixes rather than between two numbers typed to agree - the same
# reason the Waypoints scene puts them there.

scene demo
waypoint ALFA Ridge repeater | up the fire road, gate code is on the tag
waypoint CHRL Water cache

# The first row of the Nodes list is the way in, and it says how many of the roster has a
# position at all - which on a real mesh is a small fraction of it.
tab nodes
hold 1000
key a
hold 1600

# The map opens framed on everything it can place, with the crosshair wherever that leaves it -
# which here is open grid between the cluster and the far node, so the line under the map says
# there is nothing under it and the crosshair is drawn dim.
#
# Closer, then west and south onto the cluster. The line under the map fills in as the crosshair
# arrives: the node's name, how far it is from our own radio and which way, and how much of its
# position to believe.
key x
hold 900
key left
hold 700
key down 2
hold 1800

# One level closer again, on something. The grid's spacing steps down the 1-2-5 ladder with the
# zoom and the scale bar follows it, so the picture stays readable as a measurement.
key x
hold 1600

# And opening what the crosshair is on: the node's own detail, over the map rather than instead
# of it - so B lands back on the view that was panned to rather than on the list.
key a
hold 1600
key b
hold 1200

# Back to everything, which is the view the map opened on. That is what makes the first pan
# something a reader can undo.
key start
hold 1400
